+The audit subsystem's own measurements (\code{backend/app/audit/NOTES.md}) are as follows. 1,000 real \code{record()} calls take 1.35\,s, i.e., about 739 events/s with an fsync per event. Verifying a chain of 20,000 events with 190 signed checkpoints and 570 anchor copies takes 0.54\,s.
+
+We measured end-to-end upload and download through the full API stack (authentication, PDP, KMS, encryption, $R=2$ placement with read-back verification, audit) with a benchmark harness in the repository (\code{scripts/bench/}). It runs the real server in its own process on the loopback interface and streams one file per request. Providers are either the real SDK adapters against local emulators or in-memory stores. Table~\ref{tab:perf} compares 1\,MiB chunks, the framework default, with 16\,MiB chunks, the deployment setting. The two settings were measured back to back in each of five rounds, alternating which came first, and every transfer was verified byte for byte. Speed-ups are medians of the per-round ratios. One cell has four rounds because an emulator failed to start in the fifth. The host is an Intel Core Ultra 9 185H (22 logical CPUs, 15.7\,GiB RAM) running Ubuntu 24.04 under WSL2 with Python 3.12.
+
+These numbers exclude network and provider latency entirely, so they show the cost of the security pipeline and of per-chunk overhead, not achievable cloud throughput. Absolute times on this host differed by up to a factor of two between sessions; the speed-ups did not, which is why the table is built from interleaved runs. The cost is per chunk rather than per byte. Stage timers on a 100\,MiB upload with 1\,MiB chunks and in-memory providers attribute about a third of the time to SQLite commits (two metadata commits and one audit commit per chunk) and another 37\% to replication with re-hashing and read-back verification, while AES-256-GCM with the per-chunk SHA-256 accounts for 7--11\% of an upload and 9--14\% of a download. With emulators, 16\,MiB chunks make uploads of 100\,MiB and more 3.0--3.3$\times$ faster and downloads 1.7--1.8$\times$ faster. In a separate sweep from 1 to 64\,MiB, throughput plateaued between 8 and 32\,MiB and 64\,MiB chunks were slower. Downloads are decrypted into a spool file and released only after verification, so with 1\,MiB chunks the first byte arrives after roughly 60--85\% of the download time for files of 20\,MiB and more.
 
